 are the  children that are accessing free school  meals  that have contact with social care or  have got a special educational need  these children are invisible and we need  to make them visible  london schools in poorer areas are so  concerned that they're forming a new  group of heads  called reconnect london to pool ideas on  how to tackle the problem  i think at heart it has to be about the  relationships  that the young people feel they have  with adults trusted adults in school and  i think if those relationships exist  if the adults in school know how to  develop those relationships and engage  with those young people  you've got a much better chance of  getting them in it's always been  children from disadvantaged backgrounds  who are more likely to skip  school but what this report shows is  that that problem has become  more pronounced lockdown has widened the  attendance gap  and it's those kids on the fringes who  are falling  further away the reasons are complex  saskia desperately wanted to go back to  school i've lost my identity  because i haven't been very social she's  been absent from october last year to  april  because her mother was shielding with  cancer and i'm not very good at  communicating my feelings  so my mum couldn't really help me in  that sense  because i just shut down report authors  say the government shouldn't just focus  on everyone catching up but also  supporting those at risk  of falling out of education altogether  jason farrell sky news
 

